1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,935
So let's just have
this in our hands

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:04,704
while we're listening and
thinking about things,

3
00:00:04,704 --> 00:00:09,376
and let me just tell
you about this one way--

4
00:00:09,376 --> 00:00:11,311
if I take a piece of glass--

5
00:00:11,311 --> 00:00:15,515
if I take a piece of
glass, and now imagine

6
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:18,451
that I take some part of
it, like this part here.

7
00:00:21,221 --> 00:00:27,594
And in this part I'm
going to cool faster,

8
00:00:27,594 --> 00:00:33,934
and in this part here
I'm going to cool slower.

9
00:00:33,934 --> 00:00:40,974
So imagine it's a slab of glass,
and it's like the edges are

10
00:00:40,974 --> 00:00:43,276
cooled at a different rate.

11
00:00:43,276 --> 00:00:45,412
That's actually exactly
why a car window,

12
00:00:45,412 --> 00:00:47,847
if you look at it through
polarized glasses,

13
00:00:47,847 --> 00:00:49,916
has spots on it.

14
00:00:49,916 --> 00:00:52,886
Those spots, some of
you may have seen these.

15
00:00:52,886 --> 00:00:57,223
Those spots are changes,
purposeful changes

16
00:00:57,223 --> 00:01:00,727
in the mechanical properties
of the glass to keep you safe.

17
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:03,563
Why?

18
00:01:03,563 --> 00:01:08,735
If you call it slower, then
you might have some volume

19
00:01:08,735 --> 00:01:10,570
that you want.

20
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:17,043
The volume here, let's call
this Vb and let's call this Va.

21
00:01:17,043 --> 00:01:21,715
That goes along with the
graph we used before, b and a.

22
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:26,586
So it wants V--

23
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:28,121
what did I do-- b.

24
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:29,789
This wants Va.

25
00:01:29,789 --> 00:01:30,824
But look at what happens.
